    "seconds": 54,
    "summary": "The `user-service` Service was missing, so nothing could reach user-service and compose-post failed every request. I recreated the Service, and the load test now runs clean.\n\n**Root cause:** The `user-service` Deployment and its pod were healthy, but the `user-service` Service object did not exist in `social-network`. Other services resolve `user-service:9090` by name, and compose-post's Thrift client got `Temporary failure in name resolution`. That failure cascaded to nginx-thrift, which returned errors for `/wrk2-api/post/compose`.\n\n**Change:** I applied a `ClusterIP` Service named `user-service` on port 9090 (selector `service=user-service`). I copied the format of the sibling `user-mention-service` Service, including its Helm labels and annotations. It now has the endpoint `10.244.110.173:9090`.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `getent hosts user-service` resolves from the compose-post pod.\n- nginx-thrift and compose-post logged no errors after <time>, the moment the Service was created.\n- wrk2 round 5 reported no non-2xx responses (before the fix, each round had about 120 failures).\n- All pods in the namespace are `Running`.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- Helm isn't installed in this environment, so I couldn't run a Helm upgrade to restore the Service from the chart. The Service is managed by `kubectl apply`, and the next Helm upgrade may need to reconcile it. The chart's Service template should be checked to see why it was dropped.\n- The startup errors from mongo index creation (user-service, social-graph-service) and Redis \"Connection refused\" (user-timeline-service) happened before those backends were ready. They are not occurring now, so I left them alone.\n- The OpenTelemetry exporter errors (`otel-collector` DNS failures) are unrelated to the request path. Jaeger is an ExternalName pointing at `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`, which is in a different namespace and may not exist here. I didn't change it.",
    "timed_out": false,
